WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.609
Authorization logic should be consistent
enough to reuse and visible enough to

2
00:00:04.689 --> 00:00:05.118
review.

3
00:00:05.198 --> 00:00:08.632
Copy-pasted ownership checks drift: one
route compares owner ID,

4
00:00:08.712 --> 00:00:11.901
another forgets tenant ID, and a third
treats a role as global.

5
00:00:11.981 --> 00:00:16.151
A dedicated policy function or
authorization service can encode the domain rule

6
00:00:16.231 --> 00:00:20.156
once, expose a clear action such as edit
project, and support focused tests.

7
00:00:20.236 --> 00:00:23.302
The route or service still shows where the
decision occurs,

8
00:00:23.382 --> 00:00:26.448
so reviewers can see that a sensitive
operation is guarded.

9
00:00:26.528 --> 00:00:29.717
Place each responsibility where sufficient
context exists.

10
00:00:29.797 --> 00:00:32.679
Authentication middleware can establish
the subject.

11
00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:35.396
Parameter middleware can validate an
identifier.

12
00:00:35.476 --> 00:00:38.481
A repository can enforce broad tenant or
ownership scope.

13
00:00:38.561 --> 00:00:41.749
A service can coordinate object loading,
policy evaluation,

14
00:00:41.829 --> 00:00:43.301
and the business operation.

15
00:00:43.381 --> 00:00:46.509
A policy function can evaluate the
subject, action, object,

16
00:00:46.589 --> 00:00:48.674
and state without depending on Express.

17
00:00:48.754 --> 00:00:53.108
A giant global middleware usually cannot
make a complete object decision because it

18
00:00:53.188 --> 00:00:57.603
lacks the loaded record, delegated grants,
workflow state, or transaction context.

19
00:00:57.683 --> 00:00:59.829
Centralization should not become magic.

20
00:00:59.909 --> 00:01:04.079
Names such as canEditProject or
requireInvoiceApproval make the policy intent

21
00:01:04.159 --> 00:01:04.711
explicit.

22
00:01:04.791 --> 00:01:08.961
Pass both the action and relevant object
when a generic authorization service is

23
00:01:09.041 --> 00:01:09.777
appropriate.

24
00:01:09.857 --> 00:01:14.150
Return a simple decision or a controlled
denial rather than mutating the request in

25
00:01:14.230 --> 00:01:15.149
surprising ways.

26
00:01:15.229 --> 00:01:18.173
Keep policy functions side-effect free
when practical,

27
00:01:18.253 --> 00:01:22.178
and test boundary conditions such as
tenant mismatch, expired delegation,

28
00:01:22.258 --> 00:01:24.097
and disallowed state transitions.

29
00:01:24.177 --> 00:01:26.324
Coordinate the decision with the effect.

30
00:01:26.404 --> 00:01:28.243
If policy depends on mutable state,

31
00:01:28.323 --> 00:01:32.125
another request can change that state
between authorization and update.

32
00:01:32.205 --> 00:01:35.026
Transactions, conditional updates, version
checks,

33
00:01:35.106 --> 00:01:39.399
or database constraints may be needed to
preserve the condition through the write.

34
00:01:39.479 --> 00:01:43.894
Also decide whether the caller receives a
forbidden response or an existence-hiding

35
00:01:43.974 --> 00:01:45.078
not-found response.

36
00:01:45.158 --> 00:01:47.488
That response policy can reduce
disclosure,

37
00:01:47.568 --> 00:01:50.266
but both paths must be backed by the same
real denial.

38
00:01:50.346 --> 00:01:53.965
The important design outcome is a data
path in which object selection,

39
00:01:54.045 --> 00:01:57.540
explicit policy, and mutation cannot
silently bypass one another.
